re displayed in a report it  explains each recommendation in detail  okay now with that explanation out of  the way let's run a fit assessment and  see the results  here we go open terminal as anthony's  fit assessment tool installed on  after we run a single command and  authenticate our vmware environment  we'll see the assessment tool start to  rapidly scan through over 2000 vms  then uploads results to the google cloud  console  once the report has been generated  we see that we've assessed 2077 vms  15 of them were categorized as being  excellent candidates to attach to anthos  for vms with an agent  and the rest were great candidates to be  hosted on kubernetes using cooper  in particular we see detailed results  for three legacy vms  a front end a back end and a transaction  service  we'll examine a few results in more  depth  and later on the demo we'll use  anthocyanums to both shift and attach  these legacy vms to anthis's modern  kubernetes based control plane  now open the result for legacy front  service  we see that it has been categorized as  shift candidate due to its q vert  compatible application profile  open the result for the legacy  transaction service  we see that it has been categorized in  attach candidate due to the fact it  requires multiple network interfaces  okay  now it's time to generate the migration  artifacts that answers for vms were used  to take control these legacy vms  here we see a migration job called demo1  is already underway  clicking on create migration will show  us the prompt that was used to create  this job  by filling in just a few values  such as migration source  operating system type for the source vms  and the desired output for migration  like containerize or convert to a qvert  vm  one can easily create a vm migration job  examining our in-progress migration job  more closely  we can see it is currently preparing  some vm manager artifacts  switching back to the google cloud  console we see an empty table  after clicking the refresh button we see  that our anthous cluster now contains  the new qvert and istio custom resources  that are just created by analyst config  manager  we navigate to the workloads tab  we can see that the kubernetes pods  associated with the two legacy vms that  we shipped to cubert are now running as  well  clicking on one of these workloads  takes us to an overview page that shows  real-time metrics and events  clicking on the operations button  opens a side panel displays tons of  additional information  like granular metrics and logs  specifically designed to assess  application reliability  next we shift your focus to the answer  vm service mesh  here we see the entire service topology  of our example application  our example app generates client traffic  that is sent to a modern containerized  front-end service  this front-end service in turn sends  requests to a few other modern apis in  our three shifted and attached legacy  vms  the phone service is highly available  it sits behind a load balancer which  spreads traffic across five service  replicas  requests to the modern front end  made to a few particular endpoints are  automatically routed to the legacy  shifted from it  similarly  the modern frontend also routes a few  specific api calls to the legacy shifted  back-end service  the legacy attached transaction service  is called both by the shifted back-end  service and number of other modern apis  double-clicking on the shifted back-end  service  takes us to a detailed view of its local  service topology  where we can see info on its current  performance in research utilization  and lots of other stuff  more importantly  now that our legacy vm is management  we can now use anthos observability  tooling to make the old app awesome  to demonstrate this we're going to go  ahead and create an slo  ak a service level objective  first we select an availability based  sli  here we'll set our compliance period to  one week  and our performance goal to 99.5  availability  finally we'll click create  next we'll add an alert to our slo  here we'll just select all the default  settings  then add a short message  awesome we just sprinkled some google  sre magic on our legacy application  and now we've reached the end of our  demo  we've seen how antlers for vms can be  used to consistently manage legacy  applications using kubernetes  awesomeness  and slick google tooling enjoy  we hope you like the demo and with that  i'll hand it over to jennifer to tell us  how we can learn more about and through  vms  thanks armor thanks for sharing how our  customers can get started with anthos  for vms like umer said we would love to  work with you as well  anthos for vms is currently in preview  and we are working with a group of  customers as our design partners if  you're interested in our preview or  learning more please contact us at this  email  we would really love to talk some more  thank you very much have a great day and  enjoy the rest of next 21

